{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310577,"nonce":"Axyhvd0jZTUi8h+WAEOROuHg/og=","title":"2026-09-25 fab221c6 (682-\u003e686) — C.new as value, local generic fns, Dynamic-only T, bare-return lambdas, mixed numeric literals, BOM; what ...","message":"fab221c6, report-bx 686 (from 682), 0 LOST. git-bug 251ce3a (closed).\n\n- `C.new` as a value had NO lowering at all (class or abstract). Now TAST `lower_constructor_value` (expr/fields.rs) synthesizes `function(__ctor_argN..) return new C(..)` and pushes the ctor param types on `expected_lambda_params_stack`.\n- Function literals never registered their own `\u003cT\u003e`: `function rest\u003cT\u003e(..)` resolved T to something concrete, and a later generic call hit a binding CONFLICT (silent early return in infer_call_type_arguments → trap-stub:generic). Helper `function_type_parameter_map` (types/generics.rs).\n- Array literal element type = FIRST element's type; `[5, 6.2, 7]` was Array\u003cInt\u003e with boxed floats. Now Int elements widen.\n- MIR infers a lambda's return type from the generated tail register, NOT the TAST type — a TAST fix alone did nothing for `b -\u003e if (b) x() else return;`; lambda.rs now drops the tail when a value-less Return exists.\n- 4 corpus files start with a UTF-8 BOM (TestBytes/IO/Resource/Serialize); `Cannot find name 'eq'` was the BOM. Lexer skips it (pos=3, spans unchanged).\n\nFIXED 32559426 (0db5a61, 688-\u003e689): Issue9046's order dependence came from StaticSigIndex — a class declared BELOW the caller is typed on demand by `apply_declared_sig`, which lowered AST types with no method type params, so `T` became Placeholder \"T\" (the classes.rs pre-registration runs too late for it). Open: 14e397f Issue9764 `this = new C(p)` param inference + rest-args local generic reads boxes.\n\n**How to apply:** the CI Tests flake list now has two wall-clock tests (de9a32b); a 102ms-vs-100ms failure is not a regression. The mixed-literal and constructor-value bugs were found only by printing values in probes — print, don't just check trap stubs. Related: [[bugs_generic_call_type_args_never_reached_backend]], [[bugs_forward_declared_return_type_decays]].\n\n- The E0100 \"class not registered\" cluster is mostly ABSTRACT receivers. `@:op(a.b)` fell through expr_to_string's Debug fallback and `@:resolve` was never recorded — field resolution never existed. HIR `resolve_operator_call` / `resolved_type` (tast_to_hir.rs) now rewrite reads/writes; the TAST types such fields as placeholders, so the resolver's return type must be threaded into chained reads and operator lookup by hand.\n- inline_expression_deep's fallback arm (`_ =\u003e lower_expression`) leaves `this`/params UNBOUND for any kind it lacks; MIR lowers an unbound `this` to None and the enclosing STATEMENT silently vanishes (no W0020, exit 0) — 456a69c (priority:high). Interpolation arm added; FunctionCall/Block/Var/lambda still missing.\n- CI Benchmarks: compare blocks BY NAME (`bench (x)` prefix) — job order differs between runs; deltablue's 20→40ms in fab221c6 was runner noise (compile time up on every tier, all ctor params annotated).\n\n- Triage by 4 read-only subagents (4 rows each, \"reduce to a probe, name the layer\") found 16 distinct causes in 16 rows — the WRONG_ANSWER bucket does NOT cluster by message; parallel triage + separate open issues (6c324fe..fb38d22) was the productive shape.\n- The MIR inliner COPIED the callee's param type onto the CALLER's argument register (erased T =\u003e I64), silently widening the caller's own i32 arithmetic. Fixing it exposed that build_binop types results from the LEFT operand: `Int - Int64` ran in 32 bits, and TestInt64 had passed only because `==` compared truncated values too. A LOST row after a correct fix can be a second bug the first one was hiding — reproduce the failing checks in isolation before reverting.\n- Harness `t()` has no usable PosInfos (lineNumber 0); bracket failures by printing a marker per test in the injected main, not by editing test bodies (TestInt64 edits trigger 0cbca12).\n- I rebuilt while a gate's suite was running (rule break) — killed that gate and re-gated.\n\n- A 9-fix batch gained 11 but LOST 5; bisecting by patch GROUP (revert set, rebuild, run only the 16 moved rows: scratch bisect/run.sh) took 4 builds. Every LOST row came from a change applied too BROADLY (retype every `this`, unwrap every abstract, widen every Int arg); narrowing each to the case it was written for kept all gains. Several losses were rows passing by accident on raw-bit equality.\n- User (2026-09-26): \"The tests are using so much memory\" — gate's `cargo test -p compiler --tests` now runs `-j 2 --test-threads=2`; clippy `-j 2`. The Mac is shared with zyntax/caribou sessions (load hit 162).\n\n- Index summary moved from MEMORY.md (2026-10-18): 2026-09-25/26 fab221c6..8aed79b8 (682-\u003e707): `C.new` values, literal `\u003cT\u003e`, bare-return lambdas, BOM; `@:op(a.b)`/`@:resolve` in HIR. HAZARD 456a69c: an unbound `this` makes its statement SILENTLY vanish. 32559426 forward sigs keep `\u003cT\u003e`; 659782b1 inliner no longer retypes caller regs + Int/Int64 binop. Open triage issues 6c324fe..fb38d22, b3220bf.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_ctor_values_local_generics_bom` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}